<text top="171" left="117" width="97" height="27" font="0"><b>Summary</b></text>
<text top="218" left="117" width="635" height="18" font="1">Technical presentations built from code (Slidev, Marp, Mermaid, PlantUML, etc.) are typically version-</text>
<text top="238" left="117" width="661" height="18" font="1">controlled text (Markdown or DSL), so content can live alongside source code. Slidev can include external</text>
<text top="259" left="117" width="424" height="18" font="1">code snippets and render Mermaid/PlantUML diagrams via servers</text>
<text top="259" left="541" width="14" height="18" font="2">【</text>
<text top="259" left="554" width="87" height="18" font="1">42†L101-L109</text>
<text top="259" left="641" width="27" height="18" font="2">】【</text>
<text top="259" left="668" width="87" height="18" font="1">47†L154-L162</text>
<text top="259" left="756" width="14" height="18" font="2">】</text>
<text top="259" left="769" width="4" height="18" font="1">.</text>
<text top="279" left="117" width="677" height="18" font="1">Marp itself has no built-in Mermaid support – diagrams must be pre-rendered or processed via CLI engines</text>
<text top="300" left="117" width="358" height="18" font="1">(e.g. Kroki) because Marp relies on server-side rendering</text>
<text top="300" left="475" width="14" height="18" font="2">【</text>
<text top="300" left="488" width="72" height="18" font="1">27†L12-L20</text>
<text top="300" left="560" width="27" height="18" font="2">】【</text>
<text top="300" left="587" width="87" height="18" font="1">72†L178-L186</text>
<text top="300" left="674" width="14" height="18" font="2">】</text>
<text top="300" left="688" width="92" height="18" font="1">. Mermaid and</text>
<text top="320" left="117" width="673" height="18" font="1">PlantUML promote the “diagram-as-code” approach: diagrams are just text, stored and reviewed with code</text>
<text top="341" left="117" width="197" height="18" font="1">so they “never drift out of date”</text>
<text top="341" left="314" width="14" height="18" font="2">【</text>
<text top="341" left="328" width="64" height="18" font="1">39†L6-L10</text>
<text top="341" left="392" width="27" height="18" font="2">】【</text>
<text top="341" left="419" width="87" height="18" font="1">37†L120-L128</text>
<text top="341" left="506" width="14" height="18" font="2">】</text>
<text top="341" left="520" width="244" height="18" font="1">. Drift between docs and code must be</text>
<text top="362" left="117" width="530" height="18" font="1">caught with external tools or processes (e.g. docdiff flags docs stale if code changes</text>
<text top="362" left="647" width="14" height="18" font="2">【</text>
<text top="362" left="660" width="87" height="18" font="1">35†L199-L207</text>
<text top="362" left="747" width="14" height="18" font="2">】</text>
<text top="362" left="761" width="26" height="18" font="1">). To</text>
<text top="382" left="117" width="680" height="18" font="1">measure rapid comprehension of a technical change, teams should use objective user-testing methods: ask</text>
<text top="403" left="117" width="581" height="18" font="1">users to paraphrase key points immediately, or perform a relevant task while thinking aloud</text>
<text top="403" left="698" width="14" height="18" font="2">【</text>
<text top="403" left="712" width="57" height="18" font="1">81†L192-</text>
<text top="424" left="117" width="30" height="18" font="1">L194</text>
<text top="424" left="147" width="27" height="18" font="2">】【</text>
<text top="424" left="174" width="72" height="18" font="1">79†L92-L99</text>
<text top="424" left="246" width="14" height="18" font="2">】</text>
<text top="424" left="259" width="512" height="18" font="1">. Success is judged by accuracy and speed, not by self-reported understanding or</text>
<text top="445" left="117" width="81" height="18" font="1">reading time</text>
<text top="445" left="198" width="14" height="18" font="2">【</text>
<text top="445" left="212" width="87" height="18" font="1">18†L192-L200</text>
<text top="445" left="299" width="27" height="18" font="2">】【</text>
<text top="445" left="326" width="72" height="18" font="1">55†L25-L34</text>
<text top="445" left="397" width="14" height="18" font="2">】</text>
<text top="445" left="411" width="344" height="18" font="1">. Small, iterative tests (often ~5–8 users) usually suffice</text>
<text top="466" left="117" width="14" height="18" font="2">【</text>
<text top="466" left="131" width="72" height="18" font="1">60†L75-L84</text>
<text top="466" left="202" width="27" height="18" font="2">】【</text>
<text top="466" left="229" width="87" height="18" font="1">62†L106-L113</text>
<text top="466" left="316" width="14" height="18" font="2">】</text>
<text top="466" left="330" width="7" height="18" font="1">. </text>
<text top="509" left="117" width="86" height="27" font="0"><b>Findings</b></text>
<text top="555" left="162" width="312" height="18" font="1">Slidev slides are plain Markdown; developers can </text>
<text top="555" left="474" width="109" height="18" font="3"><b>import live code</b></text>
<text top="555" left="583" width="134" height="18" font="1"> from source files via </text>
<text top="557" left="723" width="49" height="16" font="4">&lt;&lt;&lt; @/</text>
<text top="579" left="162" width="121" height="16" font="4">path/to/file.js</text>
<text top="577" left="289" width="308" height="18" font="1"> so slide content always reflects the current code</text>
<text top="577" left="597" width="14" height="18" font="2">【</text>
<text top="577" left="610" width="87" height="18" font="1">42†L101-L109</text>
<text top="577" left="698" width="14" height="18" font="2">】</text>
<text top="577" left="711" width="7" height="18" font="1">. </text>
<text top="598" left="162" width="235" height="18" font="1">In Slidev, fenced Mermaid blocks are </text>
<text top="598" left="397" width="250" height="18" font="3"><b>automatically converted to diagrams</b></text>
<text top="598" left="647" width="83" height="18" font="1"> at build time</text>
<text top="598" left="730" width="14" height="18" font="2">【</text>
<text top="598" left="744" width="57" height="18" font="1">24†L230-</text>
<text top="619" left="162" width="30" height="18" font="1">L238</text>
<text top="619" left="192" width="14" height="18" font="2">】</text>
<text top="619" left="206" width="532" height="18" font="1">, and PlantUML blocks are rendered by sending code to PlantUML’s server by default</text>
<text top="640" left="162" width="109" height="18" font="1">(configurable via </text>
<text top="642" left="277" width="113" height="16" font="4">plantUmlServer</text>
<text top="640" left="396" width="4" height="18" font="1">)</text>
<text top="640" left="400" width="14" height="18" font="2">【</text>
<text top="640" left="413" width="87" height="18" font="1">47†L154-L162</text>
<text top="640" left="500" width="14" height="18" font="2">】</text>
<text top="640" left="514" width="7" height="18" font="1">. </text>
<text top="661" left="162" width="207" height="18" font="1">Marp (Markdown Presentations) </text>
<text top="661" left="369" width="229" height="18" font="3"><b>does not natively render Mermaid</b></text>
<text top="661" left="599" width="18" height="18" font="1">: a </text>
<text top="663" left="623" width="57" height="16" font="4">mermaid</text>
<text top="661" left="685" width="110" height="18" font="1"> block appears as</text>
<text top="683" left="162" width="499" height="18" font="1">literal code unless the diagram is pre-rendered or passed through a CLI engine</text>
<text top="683" left="661" width="14" height="18" font="2">【</text>
<text top="683" left="674" width="72" height="18" font="1">27†L12-L20</text>
<text top="683" left="746" width="14" height="18" font="2">】</text>
<text top="683" left="759" width="31" height="18" font="1">. The</text>
<text top="703" left="162" width="575" height="18" font="1">Marp team notes that Marp Core “does not support mermaid.js” (since it is SSR-based), and</text>
<text top="724" left="162" width="526" height="18" font="1">recommends using an external rendering service (like Kroki) for Mermaid diagrams</text>
<text top="724" left="688" width="14" height="18" font="2">【</text>
<text top="724" left="702" width="57" height="18" font="1">72†L178-</text>
<text top="745" left="162" width="30" height="18" font="1">L186</text>
<text top="745" left="192" width="27" height="18" font="2">】【</text>
<text top="745" left="219" width="87" height="18" font="1">72†L194-L198</text>
<text top="745" left="306" width="14" height="18" font="2">】</text>
<text top="745" left="320" width="7" height="18" font="1">. </text>
<text top="765" left="162" width="171" height="18" font="1">Mermaid’s design motto is </text>
<text top="765" left="333" width="128" height="18" font="3"><b>“diagrams as code”</b></text>
<text top="765" left="461" width="339" height="18" font="1">: “Your diagram is just text… The diagram lives next to</text>
<text top="786" left="162" width="332" height="18" font="1">the thing it describes — and never drifts out of date”</text>
<text top="786" left="494" width="14" height="18" font="2">【</text>
<text top="786" left="508" width="64" height="18" font="1">39†L6-L10</text>
<text top="786" left="572" width="14" height="18" font="2">】</text>
<text top="786" left="585" width="199" height="18" font="1">. This means Mermaid source is</text>
<text top="806" left="162" width="270" height="18" font="1">version-controlled alongside system code. </text>
<text top="826" left="162" width="288" height="18" font="1">PlantUML likewise treats diagrams as code: a </text>
<text top="828" left="456" width="41" height="16" font="4">.puml</text>
<text top="826" left="502" width="296" height="18" font="1"> file is kept in the source repo and regenerated</text>
<text top="847" left="162" width="591" height="18" font="1">by CI. According to PlantUML docs, the diagram “cannot silently drift out of date on some wiki</text>
<text top="868" left="162" width="316" height="18" font="1">nobody edits, because it is part of the change set”</text>
<text top="868" left="478" width="14" height="18" font="2">【</text>
<text top="868" left="491" width="87" height="18" font="1">37†L120-L128</text>
<text top="868" left="578" width="14" height="18" font="2">】</text>
<text top="868" left="592" width="204" height="18" font="1">. Version-control and CI building</text>
<text top="888" left="162" width="256" height="18" font="1">ensure diagrams stay synced with code. </text>
<text top="908" left="162" width="86" height="18" font="1">Tools exist to </text>
<text top="908" left="248" width="311" height="18" font="3"><b>detect drift between code and documentation</b></text>
<text top="908" left="558" width="238" height="18" font="1">: e.g. docdiff lets developers annotate</text>
<text top="929" left="162" width="65" height="18" font="1">code with </text>
<text top="930" left="233" width="32" height="16" font="4">@doc</text>
<text top="929" left="271" width="488" height="18" font="1"> links, then flags docs as stale if related code changes without updating them</text>
<text top="950" left="162" width="14" height="18" font="2">【</text>
<text top="950" left="176" width="87" height="18" font="1">35†L199-L207</text>
<text top="950" left="263" width="14" height="18" font="2">】</text>
<text top="950" left="276" width="490" height="18" font="1">. This ensures no claims in slides/docs are left unreviewed after code evolves. </text>
<text top="970" left="162" width="570" height="18" font="1">Comprehension depends on user background, so it must be tested empirically (not just by</text>
<text top="991" left="162" width="630" height="18" font="1">readability formulas). Nielsen Norman notes that factors like IQ and education matter, so one “must</text>
<text top="1011" left="162" width="460" height="18" font="1">test with real users” from the target audience to measure understanding</text>
<text top="1011" left="622" width="14" height="18" font="2">【</text>
<text top="1011" left="635" width="87" height="18" font="1">50†L108-L112</text>
<text top="1011" left="723" width="14" height="18" font="2">】</text>
<text top="1011" left="736" width="7" height="18" font="1">. </text>
<text top="555" left="147" width="15" height="18" font="1">1. </text>
<text top="598" left="147" width="15" height="18" font="1">2. </text>
<text top="661" left="147" width="15" height="18" font="1">3. </text>
<text top="765" left="147" width="15" height="18" font="1">4. </text>
<text top="826" left="147" width="15" height="18" font="1">5. </text>
<text top="908" left="147" width="15" height="18" font="1">6. </text>
<text top="970" left="147" width="15" height="18" font="1">7. </text>
<text top="1125" left="455" width="8" height="18" font="5">1</text>
</page>
<page number="2" position="absolute" top="0" left="0" height="1188" width="918">
	<fontspec id="6" size="14" family="IDIOZN+Noto-Sans" color="#000000"/>
<text top="110" left="162" width="12" height="18" font="1">A </text>
<text top="110" left="174" width="64" height="18" font="3"><b>cloze test</b></text>
<text top="110" left="238" width="386" height="18" font="1"> (fill-in-the-blank) is one empirical measure: if participants fill </text>
<text top="110" left="624" width="14" height="18" font="2">≥</text>
<text top="110" left="637" width="138" height="18" font="1">60% of missing words</text>
<text top="131" left="162" width="474" height="18" font="1">correctly, the text is deemed “reasonably comprehensible” to that audience</text>
<text top="131" left="636" width="14" height="18" font="2">【</text>
<text top="131" left="650" width="87" height="18" font="1">50†L124-L132</text>
<text top="131" left="737" width="14" height="18" font="2">】</text>
<text top="131" left="750" width="33" height="18" font="1">. This</text>
<text top="151" left="162" width="281" height="18" font="1">sets a benchmark for quick comprehension. </text>
<text top="171" left="162" width="252" height="18" font="1">Immediately after reading, ask users to </text>
<text top="171" left="414" width="184" height="18" font="3"><b>paraphrase or “teach back”</b></text>
<text top="171" left="598" width="187" height="18" font="1"> the content. For example: “In</text>
<text top="192" left="162" width="251" height="18" font="1">your own words, what does this mean?”</text>
<text top="192" left="413" width="14" height="18" font="2">【</text>
<text top="192" left="427" width="87" height="18" font="1">81†L192-L194</text>
<text top="192" left="514" width="14" height="18" font="2">】</text>
<text top="192" left="528" width="246" height="18" font="1">. If they can restate the key meaning, it</text>
<text top="213" left="162" width="287" height="18" font="1">proves they built the intended understanding</text>
<text top="213" left="449" width="14" height="18" font="2">【</text>
<text top="213" left="463" width="72" height="18" font="1">79†L92-L99</text>
<text top="213" left="535" width="14" height="18" font="2">】</text>
<text top="213" left="548" width="7" height="18" font="1">. </text>
<text top="233" left="162" width="27" height="18" font="1">Use </text>
<text top="233" left="189" width="123" height="18" font="3"><b>task-based checks</b></text>
<text top="233" left="312" width="445" height="18" font="1">: have users perform a relevant task based on the change and observe</text>
<text top="253" left="162" width="602" height="18" font="1">success. Task-based comprehension is defined as “whether the user acts correctly on what they</text>
<text top="274" left="162" width="79" height="18" font="1">understood”</text>
<text top="274" left="241" width="14" height="18" font="2">【</text>
<text top="274" left="255" width="72" height="18" font="1">79†L92-L99</text>
<text top="274" left="326" width="14" height="18" font="2">】</text>
<text top="274" left="340" width="433" height="18" font="1">. If the user completes the task as intended (per pre-written criteria),</text>
<text top="295" left="162" width="262" height="18" font="1">they have likely understood the material. </text>
<text top="315" left="162" width="116" height="18" font="1">Observe users via </text>
<text top="315" left="278" width="138" height="18" font="3"><b>think-aloud protocol</b></text>
<text top="315" left="416" width="324" height="18" font="1"> to catch hidden misunderstandings. By verbalizing</text>
<text top="336" left="162" width="404" height="18" font="1">thoughts, users expose any “misconceptions” about the content</text>
<text top="336" left="566" width="14" height="18" font="2">【</text>
<text top="336" left="579" width="79" height="18" font="1">62†L97-L105</text>
<text top="336" left="659" width="14" height="18" font="2">】</text>
<text top="336" left="672" width="103" height="18" font="1">. This qualitative</text>
<text top="356" left="162" width="612" height="18" font="1">insight often reveals exactly which part of the explanation was unclear. (Think-aloud tests require</text>
<text top="377" left="162" width="317" height="18" font="1">only a few participants to yield actionable findings</text>
<text top="377" left="479" width="14" height="18" font="2">【</text>
<text top="377" left="493" width="87" height="18" font="1">62†L106-L113</text>
<text top="377" left="580" width="14" height="18" font="2">】</text>
<text top="377" left="593" width="11" height="18" font="1">.) </text>
<text top="397" left="162" width="622" height="18" font="1">Measure performance (speed and accuracy) not just declared understanding. Real comprehension</text>
<text top="418" left="162" width="527" height="18" font="1">shows up in how quickly and correctly users act, not in click analytics or self-reports</text>
<text top="418" left="689" width="14" height="18" font="2">【</text>
<text top="418" left="702" width="72" height="18" font="1">52†L90-L99</text>
<text top="418" left="774" width="14" height="18" font="2">】</text>
<text top="439" left="162" width="14" height="18" font="2">【</text>
<text top="439" left="176" width="87" height="18" font="1">52†L106-L108</text>
<text top="439" left="263" width="14" height="18" font="2">】</text>
<text top="439" left="276" width="278" height="18" font="1">. For example, one practitioner notes that if </text>
<text top="439" left="554" width="14" height="18" font="2">≥</text>
<text top="439" left="567" width="233" height="18" font="1">90% of users complete a task in ~3–4</text>
<text top="460" left="162" width="367" height="18" font="1">minutes with minimal errors, comprehension is likely high</text>
<text top="460" left="529" width="14" height="18" font="2">【</text>
<text top="460" left="543" width="72" height="18" font="1">52†L90-L99</text>
<text top="460" left="615" width="14" height="18" font="2">】</text>
<text top="460" left="628" width="7" height="18" font="1">. </text>
<text top="480" left="162" width="229" height="18" font="3"><b>Avoid self-report and time proxies</b></text>
<text top="480" left="391" width="401" height="18" font="1">. Studies show self-reported reading time greatly overestimates</text>
<text top="501" left="162" width="467" height="18" font="1">actual time (by ~4× in one trial) and correlates poorly with comprehension</text>
<text top="501" left="629" width="14" height="18" font="2">【</text>
<text top="501" left="643" width="87" height="18" font="1">18†L192-L200</text>
<text top="501" left="730" width="14" height="18" font="2">】</text>
<text top="501" left="743" width="4" height="18" font="1">.</text>
<text top="521" left="162" width="636" height="18" font="1">Likewise, natural clickstream reading times have no meaningful correlation with text comprehension</text>
<text top="542" left="162" width="14" height="18" font="2">【</text>
<text top="542" left="176" width="72" height="18" font="1">55†L25-L34</text>
<text top="542" left="247" width="14" height="18" font="2">】</text>
<text top="542" left="261" width="479" height="18" font="1">. Relying on subjective or time-based metrics alone is therefore misleading. </text>
<text top="562" left="162" width="393" height="18" font="1">Use iterative small-sample testing. For rapid validation, only a </text>
<text top="562" left="555" width="110" height="18" font="3"><b>handful of users</b></text>
<text top="562" left="664" width="103" height="18" font="1"> (often ~5–8) are</text>
<text top="583" left="162" width="115" height="18" font="1">needed per round</text>
<text top="583" left="277" width="14" height="18" font="2">【</text>
<text top="583" left="290" width="72" height="18" font="1">60†L75-L84</text>
<text top="583" left="362" width="27" height="18" font="2">】【</text>
<text top="583" left="389" width="87" height="18" font="1">62†L106-L113</text>
<text top="583" left="476" width="14" height="18" font="2">】</text>
<text top="583" left="490" width="256" height="18" font="1">. Multiple small rounds (testing, revising,</text>
<text top="604" left="162" width="415" height="18" font="1">retesting) catch recurring confusion faster than a single large test</text>
<text top="604" left="577" width="14" height="18" font="2">【</text>
