<text top="719" left="117" width="106" height="27" font="0"><b>Unverified</b></text>
<text top="766" left="162" width="402" height="18" font="1">No evidence was found of any slide/diagram tool automatically </text>
<text top="766" left="564" width="98" height="18" font="3"><b>detecting drift</b></text>
<text top="766" left="662" width="97" height="18" font="1"> or mismatches</text>
<text top="786" left="162" width="627" height="18" font="1">between slide content and code beyond using general version-control or external tools. In practice,</text>
<text top="806" left="162" width="589" height="18" font="1">teams must audit changes manually or use annotations (as in docdiff) to avoid stale content. </text>
<text top="827" left="162" width="635" height="18" font="1">It is assumed (but not documented) that other diagrams-as-code tools (e.g. Graphviz, D2, etc.) follow</text>
<text top="847" left="162" width="615" height="18" font="1">similar patterns of living in repos; we did not find specific claims on their drift-detection features. </text>
<text top="885" left="117" width="103" height="27" font="0"><b>Contested</b></text>
<text top="931" left="162" width="609" height="18" font="1">We found no direct contradictions among sources. Different experts (e.g. Nielsen vs. practitioner</text>
<text top="951" left="162" width="615" height="18" font="1">blogs) set different “passing” thresholds (e.g. 60% correct in cloze vs. 90% task success), but these</text>
<text top="972" left="162" width="311" height="18" font="1">reflect context rather than factual disagreement. </text>
